  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions/Tonight:\n\nHigh pressure has exited the region this morning with southerly \nsurface winds streaming back into the Northland. Satellite shows a \nswath of cloud cover slowly moving in from the west and engulfing \nthe region. The radar does show some weak echoes across the region \nbut model soundings still show and impressive dry slot through the \nlow levels which is likely preventing any precipitation from hitting \nthe ground. \n\nA low pressure system moving across the Canadian Prairies will lead to \nan active weather pattern beginning tonight and through Wednesday. \nCurrent expectations are for an area of showers and storms to \ninitiate over the Northern Plains late this evening and travel east \nthrough the overnight hours. The better ingredients for strong to \nsevere weather remain to our west where SPC has a Marginal risk \noutlined. As these storms move east the the 12Z suite of guidance \nsuggests that they will outrun both the strongest instability and \navailable bulk shear. Some of the latest CAMs are maintaining a few \nmore vigorous cells into the early morning hours across our west, \nbut largely expect this activity to weaken into showers for Tuesday \nmorning. \n\nTuesday:\n\nOngoing showers are expected to be scattered across the region \nthrough the morning hours from the previous nights activity. Despite \nthe cloud cover and morning precipitation, we still expect ample \nrecovery of moisture in the afternoon. A warm and moist air mass \nahead of a cold front sliding in from the west will lead to the \ndevelopment of more widespread showers and storms. Modest instability \nwith dewpoints in the 60s should lead to MLCAPE in excess of 1500 \nJ/kg. Bulk shear will also be improving through the afternoon as \ndeep layer shear increases due to the upper level low moving along \nthe International Border. Pairing these together should result in an \nenvironment capable of producing strong to severe storms. The \nlatest HREF is also depicting a few impressive updraft helicity \ntracks suggesting some supercell development. Main hazards look to \nbe hail of 1.5\" possible with wind gusts of 65 mph. A tornado can't \nfully be ruled out as well with strong 3CAPE and ample surface \nvorticity ahead of the frontal boundary. Peak severe weather timing \nlooks to be between 3PM - 10PM. SPC has our region highlighted in a \nmarginal risk (threat level 1 out of 5).\n\nWednesday:\n\nThe cold front will have moved out of the region on Wednesday with \nnorthwesterly winds flowing back across the Northland. The upper \nlevel low will still be churning overhead as it scuttles towards the \nGreat Lakes. Residual moisture combined with the cyclonic flow\naloft will generate some additional showers with isolated \nrumbles of thunder. No severe weather is expected though with \nactivity tapering off after sunset. \n\nInto the Weekend:\n\nAnother stint of quiet and pleasant weather begins Thursday. \nNorthwest flow continues aloft with surface high pressure over \nManitoba expanding into the Upper Midwest. Highs will be back into \nthe 70s with a gradual warm up heading into the weekend. We may see \nanother chance at precipitation to start the weekend as impulses \naloft break down the ridge in place. Cross barrier flow through the \nNorthern Rockies may develop a low in the Dakotas that tracks across \nthe Midwest. Still plenty of disagreement in timing amongst models, \nfor now we have 20-30% PoPs for the weekend.",
